00:09:36.710 --> 00:09:38.150
[Jake] You're late man

00:09:38.150 --> 00:09:43.059
[Wren] Dude, okay. So I left at like 9:30. I know it's like 1, now. In my defense

00:09:43.060 --> 00:09:45.580
I did have to like fly the drone and check the battery

00:09:45.770 --> 00:09:50.169
Basically the second half of the ride I couldn't go faster than like five miles an hour the last two miles

00:09:50.170 --> 00:09:52.960
I was having to be like, a regular old skateboarder. This means

00:09:52.960 --> 00:09:57.999
I can't, I can't depend on this to get to work. Remember that water analogy? These two represent the battery packs I made and this

00:09:58.000 --> 00:10:01.450
represents the Boosted Board battery. The way batteries work is that they start at a certain voltage

00:10:01.450 --> 00:10:06.460
and then they drop voltages until they reach a point where they can't drop any further. For the Boosted Board battery

00:10:06.480 --> 00:10:11.840
that's about 33 and a half volts. So once the battery gets to that voltage, it no longer works.

00:10:12.440 --> 00:10:13.930
Unfortunately the two battery packs

00:10:13.930 --> 00:10:18.600
I made can continue to work down to 30 volts. As soon as we reach the bottom of this battery,

00:10:18.730 --> 00:10:23.950
we can't utilize any more power from these batteries. So I have all this energy that I can't utilize yet.

00:10:24.140 --> 00:10:25.210
So I need your help.

00:10:25.210 --> 00:10:28.239
I need to somehow make it so that I can utilize all of the energy in a

00:10:28.340 --> 00:10:31.660
smaller voltage range. Is that even possible? I don't know if that's possible.

00:10:31.790 --> 00:10:38.050
What if I used some sort of, like, boost converter to up the battery voltage over the course of time. Let's brainstorm together.

00:10:38.050 --> 00:10:42.190
Maybe we can figure out a way to make this battery system work. Thanks for watching. In the meantime,

00:10:42.190 --> 00:10:45.940
